A stronger construction is emerging from the paper’s Section 6 encoding. I can plant three perfect matchings in a cubic graph, hide them, take its line graph, and apply the paper’s exact “one colour per vertex / one occurrence per triangle” clauses. A witness is then a 3-edge-colouring; checking is linear-time, while cubic 3-edge-colourability is NP-complete. This also avoids the fatal linear-algebra shortcut that exposes many naïvely planted exact-one formulas.
